{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Exact head b391c9188460b02fccae2eafddb614dbab5fd193, artifacts 68a90fdf (cmd/gitseq-perf), 6747209a (internal/perfscenario) and e2b15773 (performance). I checked the claims by re-measuring on fixtures I prepared myself rather than by reading the tests, and every number and digest this head asserts either reproduces exactly or reproduces within run-to-run variation. One test-coverage gap is recorded below. It does not put any recorded number in doubt and the shipped code is correct; I recommend it as a child request rather than as a blocking change, and I say why.\n\nTHE NUMBERS THIS HEAD WAS ASKED FOR. The measurement gap in parent request d7073219 was that no fixture placed a checkpoint near the head of a deep sequence, so checkpoint-load cost at FIRST-PRODUCTION depth and the restart it dominates were unmeasurable, and the evidence page records them as not established. They are now established. My own runs, darwin/arm64, 18 cores, 64 GiB, load average 2.1 to 7.9, one fresh scratch per sample, every sample reporting verified_signed_checkpoint_tail with correctness digest equal to trusted digest:\n\n  checkpoint 499,745 + tail 255, depth 500,000: 70.622 s, peak RSS 21.36 GiB, response 3.07 GB\n  checkpoint  49,745 + tail 255, depth  50,000: 17.690 s and 17.755 s, peak RSS 1.81 GiB\n  checkpoint 499,745 + tail   1, depth 499,746: 57.010 s, peak RSS 19.22 GiB\n  checkpoint  49,745 + tail   1, depth  49,746:  5.924 s, peak RSS 1.83 GiB\n  checkpoint     257 + tail 255, depth     512: 11.819 s\n  checkpoint     257 + tail   1, depth     258:  0.316 s\n\nFIRST-PRODUCTION restart is 70.622 s against the 60 s budget, a miss of 17.7 percent. PREVIEW is 17.72 s mean of two against 10 s, a miss of 77 percent. Both corroborate the artifact's 72.986 s and 19.171 s: mine run a little faster on a quieter machine, and the misses are the same misses. The direction and the magnitude are not in doubt.\n\nWHERE THE FIRST-PRODUCTION COST ACTUALLY IS, WHICH IS NEW. The tail-1 cases isolate checkpoint load from tail fold, and the decomposition is clean and additive:\n\n  checkpoint load: 5.924 s at 49,745 records and 57.010 s at 499,745, that is 0.1191 and 0.1141 ms per checkpointed record. Linear in checkpoint size, as expected.\n  tail-255 fold: 11.503 s, 11.766 s and 13.612 s at checkpoint depths 257, 49,745 and 499,745, that is 45.11, 46.14 and 53.38 ms per tail record.\n  additivity: predicted 17.427 s versus measured 17.690 s at 50,000, and 68.513 s versus 70.622 s at 500,000, within 1.5 and 3.1 percent.\n\nSo at FIRST-PRODUCTION, checkpoint load is 57.010 s of the 70.622 s, 80.7 percent, and the tail-255 fold is 13.612 s, 19.3 percent. This matters to the pending regression work on child 08cb8b59 and I want it recorded plainly: eliminating the whole 46 ms per tail record regression would take 500,000-record restart from 70.6 s to about 57.0 s, which lands inside the 60 s budget with under 5 percent of margin and nothing left over. At FIRST-PRODUCTION the tail is not the thing to optimize; checkpoint materialization is. That conclusion was unreachable before this head existed, and it is the reason the head is worth having.\n\nREGRESSION CROSS-CHECK against child 08cb8b59, which measured tail restart at 11.95 s at depth 512. I reproduce 11.819 s for the same case, 1.1 percent apart. The per-tail-record cost is depth-independent to first order, 45.11 then 46.14 ms per record as the checkpoint grows from 257 to 49,745 records, a 2.3 percent drift across a hundredfold increase in checkpoint size. It is not perfectly flat: at 499,745 it rises to 53.38 ms per record, 18 percent above the shallow figure. I did not chase that, but the plausible reading is heap and GC pressure at a 21 GiB live projection rather than any dependence on depth as such, and it is small next to the effect it sits inside. The regression model carried forward from depth 512 holds at three orders of magnitude more depth.\n\nFIXTURE HONESTY, which is the question that decides whether any of the above means anything.\n\n(a) Production writer, yes. Prepare rewinds the sequence ref to the retained depth and then calls the ordinary app.Workspace.Snapshot. The checkpoint is published by the normal path through checkpointOptions, signed with the workspace sequencer key. There is no bespoke checkpoint writer anywhere in the fixture code and none is reachable from it. The state a sample then measures, a signed checkpoint at D-255 with the head at D, is exactly what production leaves behind after 255 appends past a checkpoint, so the measured cold path is the real one.\n\n(b) Production invalidation, yes, and it does not change what is measured. reader.InvalidateCheckpoint is the same exported method that gs checkpoint-clear gives an operator: it removes the local pointer file and resets the checkpoint ref to genesis so the next ordinary read is a cold audit. It is not a test-only shortcut and it is not a private door into the fixture. I checked the thing that would actually matter if it were, by preparing the same plan with and without the invalidation: the logical digest is identical either way, d9551bc67fde66e7a5760177ff7e92368b96f18e8354e7f82a8b88209de53e17, so the sequence being measured is the same sequence. What changes is only the cost of building it, and that change is the point: 1.719 s with the invalidation against 27.010 s without, 15.7 times, at a fixture only 513 records deep with two retained checkpoints. Extrapolated to the 499,745 checkpoint at the measured tail rate, the inherited tail scan the invalidation avoids would be some hours. The claim in artifact 6747209a is accurate and, if anything, understated.\n\n(c) The snapshot_source assertion is present in the measured path and is load-bearing. internal/perfscenario/run.go line 453 fails the sample outright unless the source is exactly verified_signed_checkpoint_tail, so a silent fall back to a full audit cannot be recorded as a checkpoint restart. This is the trap the earlier tail child taught, and the guard predates this head rather than being added for it; what this head does is bring the two deep cases under it. All six of my samples reported verified_signed_checkpoint_tail, and in all six the correctness digest equalled the trusted digest.\n\nINDEPENDENT REPRODUCTION OF THE ARTIFACT'S OWN IDENTIFIERS. I built a 500,000-record fixture from scratch and got the same logical digest the artifact records, de41b92caf72003424955346fd5634372c53b69dd5ee4b9e88fdf3a3af71b5d5, and the same three retained checkpoint depths, 257, 49,745 and 499,745. Contract validation returns cf0af42a30d84043e35013a6caf515d6481e55e64dc09c409d18fb185cbbd0ab, exactly the digest the artifact names. Fixture preparation took 22.0 s at depth 50,000 and 4 m 05 s at depth 500,000.\n\nOne correction to the record, minor and not this head's doing. The exact digest is not reproducible and should not be read as a fixture identity. I got 1e74664646bc7f5c05d103270583db9d05dc89c407098cbc25e415c6664e3070 where the artifact records 089d2cd893cdf34e3b3ed2a9543f5d405bc9f876efbaadc4678485607f45c41d, and three preparations of one identical small plan gave three different exact digests, 0eaf0b13, e4eb193d and bce4efc7, while the logical digest was stable across all three. Signed checkpoint commits carry per-run material, so the exact digest witnesses one run and cannot be re-derived by anyone. It predates this head, nothing depends on it -- ensureFixtures matches cached fixtures on generator version, seed, shape, depth and actor count, not on this digest -- and I am not asking for a change. I am asking that it not be cited in future as though a second party could check it. The logical digest is the one that carries that weight, and it does carry it: it matched exactly.\n\nMUTATIONS. I ran four against a separate worktree at the same commit, leaving the reviewed tree untouched.\n\n  Dropping the tail subtraction in checkpointDepths, so the checkpoint is no longer near the head: KILLED, TestCheckpointDepthsAreUniqueAndSorted fails at all three depths.\n  Moving the 500,000 case to 400,000: KILLED, by TestCheckpointDepthsAreUniqueAndSorted and by TestFullTierIncludesNearHeadCheckpointCases.\n\nBoth mutations artifact 68a90fdf claims are killed are genuinely killed, at every depth, and the second one dies twice. The claim is accurate.\n\n  Removing the InvalidateCheckpoint call: SURVIVES the whole suite. I am not troubled by this one. The guard is self-evidencing, because its absence multiplies preparation time by 15.7 even at depth 513 and would make the 500,000 fixture take hours; and a test that asserted preparation was fast would be a timing-dependent test, which is worse than the gap. Noted, not asked for.\n  Forcing Materialize to always take the shallowest retained checkpoint, so the measured restart runs far from the head: SURVIVES go test ./cmd/... ./internal/... in full. This is the gap.\n\nTHE GAP, AND WHY IT IS NOT A BLOCK. Nothing in the tree pins that a sample labelled tail 255 actually loaded a checkpoint 255 records from the head. The recorded checkpoint_tail comes from the request options rather than from the loaded checkpoint; run.go asserts the source but not the distance; the lane's per-sample validation is validateActorCount and nothing else; and the near-head assertion added to fixture_test checks source and final depth, both of which stay true when the checkpoint is far away and the tail fold is long. So the central invariant of this head, that these are near-head measurements, is asserted nowhere.\n\nI approve anyway, for three reasons and I want them on the record so this is not read as indulgence. First, the shipped code cannot get it wrong: Materialize looks up Checkpoints[Depth-Tail] by exact string key and hard-errors when it is absent, so there is no fallback that could silently substitute another checkpoint, and my mutation had to hardcode a constant to break it. Second, the numbers this head records do not depend on the missing test, because I reproduced them on fixtures I prepared myself and every sample carries the source assertion and matching digests; a test failing later cannot retroactively make 70.622 s wrong. Third, the exposure is future decay rather than present defect, and the plausible decay -- somebody adding a nearest-shallower-checkpoint fallback to make fixtures more reusable -- would change restart at 500,000 from 70 s to roughly six hours, which is not a failure mode that hides. Blocking correct, verified and hard-won measurement for one two-line assertion would cost a full cycle and buy very little. The proportionate instrument is a child request, and I recommend one: assert after Materialize that the checkpoint ref equals manifest.Checkpoints[Depth-Tail], which kills the mutation outright.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are the measurement and evidence lane only: cmd/gitseq-perf for tier and case selection, internal/perfscenario for fixture preparation and the scenario runner, and performance/contract-v1.json as the gitseq.performance/v1 evidence contract. docs/reference/architecture.md does not describe this lane at all -- I grepped it and the only hit is an unrelated sentence about interpreters -- so no page on it changes and none needs updating in this head. The layer contracts it could have disturbed are untouched, and I checked that as a fact rather than as a reading: git diff between the merge base and the exact head across internal/app, internal/kernel, internal/service, cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp is zero lines. Actor signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature and bound verification, fold semantics, trusted-versus-projected digest equality and checkpoint writing are all exactly as main has them, which is what \"no runtime verification change or inline optimization\" asked for and what it got. The one contract that does move is the evidence contract's own case set: schema_version is unchanged, two checkpoint cases are added, and the validation digest moves from c217129e to cf0af42a. Nothing in the repository cites either digest, so nothing is left dangling.\n\nThe merge base is main's current tip adda28c8, and the diff is four files, 66 insertions and 8 deletions, of which the only non-test production change is the eight-line invalidation in internal/perfscenario/fixture.go. Tier bounding is preserved and I measured it rather than trusting it: smoke selects 20 cases to a maximum depth of 267, standard 49 cases to 10,000, full 73 cases to 500,000, and only the full tier picks up the two new checkpoint cases.\n\nOne operational consequence that the artifacts do not state and that whoever runs the full tier should know. The contract asks for 20 repetitions and 1 warmup for checkpoint_restart, so each new case costs 21 samples: about 25 minutes for the 500,000 case and about 6 minutes for the 50,000 one, roughly 31 minutes added to a full-tier run, on top of 4 minutes of fixture preparation, and the 500,000 samples need a machine with more than 21.4 GiB free. The per-sample timeout is 300 s against a 70.6 s sample, so there is 4.2 times headroom and no timeout risk. This is a cost, not a defect -- the full tier is the heavy tier and this is what measuring FIRST-PRODUCTION costs -- but it is worth stating so nobody meets it as a surprise in CI.\n\nSECURITY. No trust or authority boundary is crossed and no production trust surface is touched. Each fixture is a wholly separate repository built by app.Init inside its own directory, with a freshly generated genesis, sequencer key and operator key; the workroom's own keys, refs and log are never read or written, and Materialize rewrites the sequencer key path into each sample's own meta directory so a sample cannot reach outside itself. Every measured restart performs the ordinary signature and bound verification -- that is precisely what the 57 s of checkpoint load is spending its time on -- and the source assertion at run.go:453 means a sample cannot pass by skipping verification, which is the one pressure a performance target puts on a kernel and the door this harness keeps shut. The fixture writer's final head is independently signature-checked through Git as a separate format boundary. The new inputs are two integers in a contract file that is validated before use. No untrusted input, no secret handling, no new bound, no new failure mode. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I looked for fluff and did not find any worth cutting. Eight lines with a comment that explains why they exist, two contract entries, and tests that assert the selection at three depths is an unusually lean way to buy a measurement this large, and the change reuses the existing checkpoint depth derivation, the existing tier gate, the existing source assertion and the existing fixture writer rather than adding parallel machinery beside any of them. The one addition I would make is the two-line materialization assertion described above, which adds coverage rather than surface. The one subtraction I considered and reject is the invalidation itself, since removing it is what would create a bespoke slow path.\n\nGATES, at the exact head with a clean tree. go build ./... clean. go vet ./... clean. go test ./... -count=1 passing. Focused packages re-run with -count=1: cmd/gitseq-perf and internal/perfscenario both ok. gitseq-perf validate returns valid gitseq.performance/v1 cf0af42a30d84043e35013a6caf515d6481e55e64dc09c409d18fb185cbbd0ab. git status in the reviewed worktree is empty before and after every mutation, and all mutation work was done in a separate worktree at the same commit.\n\nI did not merge, did not edit any tracked file at the reviewed head, and changed no branch or ref.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e2b157735e785d28f9235ed14aea37fa48c771ca","head":"b391c9188460b02fccae2eafddb614dbab5fd193","verdict":"approved"}}